$\underbrace{\cos(~\overbrace{5x-9}^{\text{inner}}~)}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $\cos(5x-9)=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{5x-9} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=\cos(x)&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{5}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{-\sin(x)}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}[\cos(5x-9)]&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{-\sin(\goldD{5x-9})} \cdot \maroonD{5}
\\\\
&=-5\sin(5x-9)
\end{align}$
